      const char *const Page49Pieces[] =
      {
         "Migrating to a different database backend\n=========================================\n\nHow to move an existing installation from one of the four supported databases to\nanother \xe2""\x80""\x94"" in practice, off **SQL Server Compact**, which the installer still\nchooses by default and which Microsoft stopped maintaining a long time ago.\n\nThere is no migration tool, and that is not an omission. Backup and restore are\nalready backend-agnostic, so the migration is three operations you already have:\nback up, repoint, restore. What was missing was this document.\n\nRead [Upgrading.md](Upgrading.md) instead if you are moving to a newer *release*\nof the server. That is a different operation and it keeps your backend.\n\nThe short version\n-----------------\n\n1. Stop the service.\n2. Take a backup with **domains, settings and messages** selected.\n3. Create an empty database on the new backend and point `hMailServer.ini` at it.\n4. Start the service and restore the backup.\n\nYour mail files never move. Nothing is exported to an intermediate format you\nhave to trust.\n\nWhy this works\n--------------\n\nWorth understanding before you run it, because it tells you what can and cannot\ngo wrong.\n\n**Nothing in the archive is tied to a database.** Neither `BackupExecuter` nor\n`BackupRestorer` looks at the database type \xe2""\x80""\x94"" there is no branch on it anywhere in\neither. The archive is XML plus, optionally, the message files.\n\n**The XML carries no identity values.** `Account::XMLStore` writes `Name`,\n`Password`, `MaxAccountSize` and the rest, and no account id; `XMLLoad` sets none.\nOn restore, `SaveObject` sees an object whose id is `0`, inserts it, and lets the\nnew database assign its own. Relationships are rebuilt through the object graph \xe2""\x80""\x94""\naccounts under their domain, folders under their account, messages under their\nfolder \xe2""\x80""\x94"" not by raw id. So the fact that MSSQL and PostgreSQL will hand out\ncompletely different numbers than SQL CE did is not something the restore has to\ncope with. It never sees the old ones.\n\n**The message store on disk is addressed by name, not by id.** A message file\nlives at `<DataFolder>\\<domain>\\<account>\\<first two characters of the guid>\\<guid>.eml`,\nand a public-folder message at `<DataFolder>\\.Public_Folder\\<xx>\\<guid>.eml`.\nThose paths are built from the domain and account *names* and the file's own guid.\nChange every id in the database and every file is still exactly where the server\nwill look for it.\n\nThat is the whole of it. The migration is possible because nothing\nidentity-shaped crosses the archive boundary, which is also why it can cross a\nbackend boundary.\n\nChoosing a target\n-----------------\n\n| Target | Choose it when |\n|---|---|\n| **MS SQL Server** (including Express and LocalDB) | You already run SQL Server, or you want the strongest transaction support of the four. Note that the session runs at READ UNCOMMITTED. Install the Microsoft OLE DB Driver for SQL Server: through its provider, MSOLEDBSQL, the whole session is encrypted by default, the certificate unchecked, and `TlsMode=verify-full` under `[Database]` checks it against the Windows certificate store; without it the server falls back to SQLOLEDB, which encrypts only the login unless `TlsMode` asks for more ([DatabaseTls.md](DatabaseTls.md)). |\n| **PostgreSQL** | You want the best-behaved backend in this codebase. It is the only one of the four where DDL is transactional, so a failed schema upgrade rolls back cleanly. To require and verify TLS to it, set `TlsMode=verify-full` and `TlsCaFile=<path to the CA file>` under `[Database]` - or libpq's own `PostgreSQLSslMode` and `PostgreSQLSslRootCert`, which win when set; without them libpq encrypts when the server offers it and verifies nothing ([DatabaseTls.md](DatabaseTls.md)). |\n| **MySQL / MariaDB** | You already run it. It must be MySQL 5.7.9 or MariaDB 10.2.2 or later, and every table is `utf8mb4` - see [MySQL and MariaDB](#mysql-and-mariadb) below. Make sure every table ends up InnoDB \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the server only issues transactions if they al",
         "l report it at connect time, and silently does not if any table does not. The connection is encrypted whenever the server offers TLS, and made without it when the server has none; `TlsMode=verify-full` and `TlsCaFile` under `[Database]` require TLS and check the server's certificate ([DatabaseTls.md](DatabaseTls.md)). Until 25 September 2026 it was never encrypted through MariaDB Connector/C - the bundled client on Windows and Ubuntu's on Linux - whatever this page said. |\n| **SQL Server Compact** | Only if you are migrating *to* a test rig. It is the default and it is the one real dependency liability in this tree; see the SQL Server Compact row in [Roadmap.md](../../Roadmap.md) for the three defects that are specific to it. |\n\nThe schema is created for you either way \xe2""\x80""\x94"" you do not need to run the SQL scripts\nby hand.\n\n### MySQL and MariaDB\n\n**MySQL 5.7.9 or MariaDB 10.2.2, or later.** Since schema 6102 every table is\n`utf8mb4` under `utf8mb4_general_ci`, InnoDB, with `ROW_FORMAT=DYNAMIC`, and a key\nover a `varchar(255)` - an account's address, a folder's name - is 1020 bytes at\nfour bytes a character. That is more than an InnoDB key part may be in the older\nCOMPACT rows (767 bytes) and more than a whole MyISAM key (1000), and those two\nreleases are the first whose InnoDB, as it ships, takes a key part of up to 3072\nbytes and makes its tables DYNAMIC. The longest key in the schema is 1060 bytes.\nThe schema was proven on MySQL 5.7.44 and 8.4.11 and on MariaDB 10.6.28, 11.4.13\nand 11.8.9 on 25 September 2026. On an older server as it ships, the create script\nstops at its first table, and an upgrade from 6.3.3 at the first step, 6040 to\n6041, whose opening statement makes a probe table with such a key (6101 opens with\nthe same probe, and 6102 with `hm_accounts`) - with error 1071, \"Specified key was\ntoo long; max key length is 767 bytes\", on a MySQL 5.7 set up as 5.6 shipped - and\nthe upgrade has then changed nothing.\n\n**Why `utf8mb4`.** MySQL's `utf8` is `utf8mb3`: three bytes a character, so nothing\noutside Unicode's Basic Multilingual Plane fits in it - no emoji, and none of the\nCJK Extension B characters some names are written in. The server has always spoken\n`utf8mb4` to MySQL (`SET NAMES utf8mb4`), so before schema 6102 such a character in\na contact, an address book, a folder, a subject in the message index, a webmail\nsetting or a signature was refused with error 1366 (\"Incorrect string value\") under\nstrict mode, the default since MySQL 5.7.5 and MariaDB 10.2.4, and was made a\nquestion mark where strict mode is off. SQL Server, SQL Server Compact and\nPostgreSQL always stored it.\n\n**Why `utf8mb4_general_ci`.** It is the twin of `utf8_general_ci`, the collation\nthe tables had: case-insensitive and accent-insensitive, and the same answer for\nevery character a `utf8` table could hold, so an address, a name or a folder\ncompares as it did and no unique index sees a duplicate it did not see before. The\nUnicode collations would not: `utf8mb4_unicode_ci` takes `\xc3""\x9f""` for `ss` and a\nfull-width letter for its ordinary one, and `utf8mb4_0900_ai_ci` exists only on\nMySQL 8 and counts trailing spaces. What `utf8mb4_general_ci` does not do is tell\ncharacters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane apart: it gives them all one\nweight. Two address books of one account called `Family \xf0""\x9f""\x91""\xaa""` and `Family \xf0""\x9f""\x8e""\x89""` are one\nname to its unique index, and the second is refused as a duplicate - as it was\nrefused before, when neither could be stored at all. Three columns compare exactly\n(`utf8mb4_bin`), as they did in `utf8_bin`: a known sender's address, the address\nan automatic reply went to, and the domain of a day's statistics.\n\n**Creating the database yourself.** Make it `utf8mb4` too, and give the account the\nserver connects with every privilege on it:\n\n```sql\nCREATE DATABASE hmailserver CHARACTER SET utf8mb4 COLLATE utf8mb4_general_ci;\nGRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON hmailserver.* TO 'hmailserver'@'%';\n```\n\nEvery table names its own character set, so a database made w",
         "ith another default\nstill works, but the upgrade steps create, alter and index tables, and the 6102\nstep also changes the database's own default character set, which needs the ALTER\nprivilege on the database. A database the server creates itself (DBSetup, or\n`hmailserver --create-database`) is made `utf8mb4` already.\n\nBefore you start\n----------------\n\n**Take a filesystem-level backup of the whole installation as well.** Not because\nthe restore is unreliable, but because it is the only thing that will get you back\nif you discover a problem after the old database has been retired. See\n[Rolling back](#rolling-back).\n\n**Check that your messages are all inside the data folder.** Backup refuses to run\nif any message row points at a file outside `DataFolder` \xe2""\x80""\x94"" it fails with \"All\nmessages are not located in the data folder\". This only happens on installations\nwhere messages were imported with absolute paths. If it fires, fix those rows\nbefore going any further; the migration is not the place to discover it.\n\n**Note your current settings.** Open `hMailServer.ini` (normally\n`C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Bin\\hMailServer.INI`) and keep a copy. The\n`[Database]` section is the part you are about to change:\n\n```ini\n[Database]\nType=MSSQLCE\nServer=\nDatabase=hMailServer\nUsername=\nPassword=\nPort=0\nInternal=1\n```\n\nThe administrator password lives in this file too, not in the database, so it\ncarries over untouched.\n\nThe procedure\n-------------\n\n### 1. Stop the service\n\n```powershell\nStop-Service hMailServer\n```\n\nWait for it to actually stop before continuing. A backup taken while mail is being\ndelivered is a backup of a moving target.\n\nRestart it for the backup itself \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the backup runs through the running server \xe2""\x80""\x94""\nbut stop accepting new mail first if you can, either by stopping the SMTP TCP/IP\nport in the administration tool or by holding the traffic upstream. Anything that\narrives after the backup and before the cutover is delivered into the *old*\ndatabase and will not be in the new one.\n\n### 2. Take the backup\n\nIn the Control Panel, under **Maintenance \xe2""\x86""\x92"" Backup & restore**, tick all three of\n**Back up domains**, **Back up messages** and **Back up settings**, set a destination\nwith room for it, and click **Start backup now**. Or from a script:\n\n```powershell\n$app = New-Object -ComObject hMailServer.Application\n$app.Authenticate(\"Administrator\", \"<password>\")\n$b = $app.Settings.Backup\n$b.BackupDomains  = $true\n$b.BackupSettings = $true\n$b.BackupMessages = $true\n$b.Destination    = \"D:\\hmail-migration\"\n$app.BackupManager.StartBackup()\n```\n\nThe archive lands in the destination folder as `HMBackup <local time>.7z`. That\none file is the whole backup \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the `hMailServerBackup.xml` index you may see\nappear next to it during the run is written into the archive and then deleted.\n\nAll three of domains, settings and messages are required. The restore refuses\ncombinations that would delete something and put nothing back \xe2""\x80""\x94"" restoring domains from a backup that has none\nwould remove every domain, account and alias on the server, so it will not do it\nand will tell you why.\n\n**If your mail store is large, use the database-only mode.** Set\n`BackupMessagesDBOnly` to `1` before both the backup and the restore \xe2""\x80""\x94"" in the\nControl Panel, over the REST API (`PUT /api/v1/settings/ini/BackupMessagesDBOnly`\nwith `1`), or with `hmctl ini set BackupMessagesDBOnly 1` \xe2""\x80""\x94"" and restart the\nservice, because it is read at start. It is stored in the database, which is the\nsettings store since schema 6042, and is not typed into `hMailServer.ini`: a line\nadded to the file is taken into the store only while the store has no row for it\n(a setting never stored, or one deleted since), and while it has one, an edit to\nthe file is named in the error log (`HM5804`) and put back. This stores and restores the message *rows* \xe2""\x80""\x94"" which is what\nyou are actually migrating \xe2""\x80""\x94"" while leaving the `.eml` files alone on disk. On an\ninstallation with a few hundred gigabytes of mail this is the difference betwe",
         "en\nminutes and most of a day, and since the data folder is not moving, copying it out\nand back achieves nothing.\n\nThe setting has to be identical for the backup and the restore, and the restore\nruns against the new database, which has a settings store of its own. It comes\nacross without being set again, the way every setting does (see step 4): the new\ndatabase's store starts empty, and the first start against it adopts the\n`[Settings]` section of `hMailServer.ini`, which is the server's copy of the old\nstore and has held `BackupMessagesDBOnly=1` since the moment it was stored. Check it\non the new database before you restore (`hmctl ini get BackupMessagesDBOnly`, or the\nControl Panel); if the old server named it in `HM5806` or `HM5803` - its line could\nnot be written into the file - set it again and restart. Remove it afterwards, in\nthe same place, so your ordinary scheduled backups go back to including the files.\n\n### 3. Create the new database\n\nCreate an **empty** database on the target server, and a login with rights to\ncreate tables in it. Do not create any tables \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the next step does that. On MySQL\nor MariaDB, make it `utf8mb4`, as [MySQL and MariaDB](#mysql-and-mariadb) shows.\n\nThen edit `hMailServer.ini` to point at it:\n\n```ini\n[Database]\nType=MSSQL\nServer=sql01.example.com\nDatabase=hMailServer\nUsername=hmailserver\nPassword=<password>\nPort=0\nInternal=0\n```\n\n`Type` is one of `MSSQL`, `MYSQL`, `PostgreSQL` or `MSSQLCE`, matched\ncase-insensitively. Set `Internal=0` for anything other than the embedded engine.\nLeave `Port=0` to use the backend's default.\n\nNow create the schema by running the database setup tool from the installation's\n`Bin` folder:\n\n```powershell\n& \"C:\\Program Files\\hMailServer\\Bin\\DBSetupQuick.exe\"\nif ($LASTEXITCODE -ne 0) { throw \"Database setup failed with exit code $LASTEXITCODE\" }\n```\n\nIt reads the connection details you just wrote, sees no database, and creates one\nat the current schema version. It returns a non-zero exit code if it fails \xe2""\x80""\x94""\nthat is what the installer checks \xe2""\x80""\x94"" so it is safe to run from a script.\n\n`DBSetup.exe`, in the same folder, is the interactive wizard and does the same\njob with prompts. Use it if you would rather enter the connection details in a\ndialog than edit the ini file by hand; it writes the same `[Database]` section.\n\n### 4. Restore\n\nWhen the server first starts against the new database it finds the settings store\nempty and adopts every line of the `[Settings]` section of `hMailServer.ini` into\nit - the server's copy of the old store, as of the last time a setting was written\ninto the file - and says so in the application log (\"setting(s) from the\n[Settings] section of hMailServer.INI are now stored in the database\"). A setting\nthe old server could not write into the file, which it reported as `HM5806` or\n`HM5803`, is not in that copy. Restoring with **Back up settings**, below, writes\nthe archived settings over the adopted ones either way.\n\nStart the service and restore the archive:\n\n```powershell\nStart-Service hMailServer\n\n$app = New-Object -ComObject hMailServer.Application\n$app.Authenticate(\"Administrator\", \"<password>\")\n$backup = $app.BackupManager.LoadBackup(\"D:\\hmail-migration\\HMBackup 2026-08-13 220511.7z\")\n$backup.RestoreDomains  = $true\n$backup.RestoreMessages = $true\n$backup.RestoreSettings = $true\n$backup.StartRestore()\n```\n\nThe server restarts itself at the end of a restore. That is expected.\n\nVerifying it worked\n-------------------\n\nDo all of these. The failure mode worth catching is a *partial* restore, which\nlooks like a working server to anyone who only checks that it starts.\n\n- **Counts match.** Domains, accounts, aliases and distribution lists, compared\n  against what you wrote down before you started.\n- **Mail is visible.** Log in over IMAP as a real account and check that the\n  folder list and the message counts are what they were. This is the one that\n  proves the message rows and the files found each other again.\n- **A message opens.** Fetch a whole m",
         "essage body, not just a header \xe2""\x80""\x94"" that\n  proves the row's file path resolves on disk under the new ids.\n- **Settings survived.** SSL certificates, TCP/IP ports, IP ranges, routes,\n  global rules and the anti-spam lists. These are the part of the backup that is\n  easiest not to notice is missing.\n- **Send and receive.** One message in, one message out.\n- **The error log is empty.** `hmailserver_ERROR_<date>.log` in the log folder.\n  Anything in it that appeared during the restore is worth reading before you\n  retire the old database.\n\nRolling back\n------------\n\nUntil you delete the old database, rollback is exact and takes a minute: stop the\nservice, put the original `[Database]` section back in `hMailServer.ini`, start\nthe service. The old database is untouched by everything above \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the migration\nonly ever read from it.\n\nThe settings come back with it. The file's `[Settings]` section now holds what\nthe new server stored, so at that start each setting changed since the cutover is\nnamed in the error log as `HM5804` and put back to the old database's value -\nwhich is the rollback working, not a fault - and a setting first stored on the new\nserver, which the old database has no row for, is adopted into it.\n\n**Keep it until you are satisfied**, and remember that any mail that arrived after\nthe cutover exists only in the new database. That is the one thing rolling back\nloses, and it is why the cutover is worth doing during a quiet window with\ninbound traffic held.\n\nThere is no downgrade path for the *schema*, but that does not apply here: you are\nnot changing schema version, only which server holds it.\n\nKnown sharp edges\n-----------------\n\n**Any mail delivered between the backup and the cutover is lost to the new\ndatabase.** Nothing in the process merges the two. Hold inbound traffic, or accept\nthe gap knowingly.\n\n**Ids change, and anything of yours that stored one will break.** Nothing inside\nthe server depends on id continuity, which is the entire basis of this\ndocument \xe2""\x80""\x94"" but if you have external scripts, reporting or an integration that\nrecorded `hm_accounts.accountid` or `hm_domains.domainid`, those values will not\nmean the same thing afterwards. Key on the address or the domain name instead.\n\n**The SQL log device's table is not part of the backup.** If your logging device is\nSQL (`Logging.Device = hLogDeviceSQL`, the Control Panel's Logging page), historical\nlog rows stay in the old database. Export them separately if you need them; they are\nordinary rows in `hm_log`.\n\n**MySQL and SQL Server Compact commit DDL as it executes.** If the schema\ncreation in step 3 fails halfway on those two, the database is left partly built\nrather than rolled back. Drop it and start step 3 again rather than re-running the\ntool over the remains.\n\nWhat this does not cover\n------------------------\n\nMoving the *data folder* to different storage, which is a separate operation and\nis not coupled to the backend at all \xe2""\x80""\x94"" the path is `[Directories] DataFolder`, and\nthe only requirement is that the service account can reach it.\n",
         nullptr
      };

